<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Paardeberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1900">1900</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Paardeberg was fought from 18 to 27 February 1900 on the Modder River, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>. British forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-roberts-1st-earl-roberts">Lord Roberts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Lord Kitchener</a> surrounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-cronje">Piet Cronjé</a>'s retreating army and, after a costly assault and a siege of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> laager, forced about 4,000 men to surrender. The defeat opened the way for the British capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> in March 1900.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-paardeberg.jpg" alt="Battle of Paardeberg" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canadian_Infantry_at_Paardeberg,_1900.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Lord Methuen's advance to relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> had been halted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-cronje">Piet Cronjé</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koos-de-la-rey">Koos de la Rey</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magersfontein">Battle of Magersfontein</a>. Field Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-roberts-1st-earl-roberts">Lord Roberts</a>, appointed to command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in December 1899 in succession to General Buller, gathered reinforcements between the Orange and Modder rivers and from 11 February 1900 sent Major General John French's cavalry around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> left. After exhausting marches across the Riet and the Modder, French reached Kimberley on the evening of 15 February, but his horses were worn out.</p><p>The same day Cronjé, with some 5,000 Transvaalers and Free Staters and many of their families in slow ox-wagons, left Jacobsdal for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>. On 17 February, as the convoy began crossing the Modder at Paardeberg Drift, 1,500 of French's mounted troops opened fire on it from the north. Instead of brushing them aside, Cronjé formed a laager and dug in on the river bank, letting the British gather about 15,000 men with far more artillery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>With Roberts ill, his chief of staff Lieutenant General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Herbert Kitchener</a> overruled Lieutenant General Thomas Kelly-Kenny's plan for a siege and on 18 February launched uncoordinated frontal assaults. By nightfall 24 officers and 279 men had been killed and 59 officers and 847 men wounded, the most severe British reverse of the war, known as Bloody Sunday. Christiaan De Wet seized the lightly held Kitchener's Kopje to the south-east, opening a way out for Cronjé.</p><p>Roberts arrived on 19 February and refused Cronjé a truce to bury the dead. The British then shelled the laager with more and more guns, killing almost every horse, mule and ox, while Cronjé refused to break out. De Wet finally abandoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kopje">kopje</a> shortly before Roberts had planned to withdraw. On the night of 26 February the Royal Canadian Regiment, helped by Royal Engineers, dug trenches close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> lines, and on 27 February Cronjé surrendered with some 4,019 men and 50 women.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The surrender, on the nineteenth anniversary of the British defeat at Majuba, was the first major British victory of the war, and about a tenth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a>' whole army were now prisoners. Thomas Pakenham judged that Cronjé's blunders had outmatched those of Kitchener and Roberts; the South African historian J. H. Breytenbach replied that Cronjé's 4,000 men with five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> had been trapped by about 40,000 soldiers with 100. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, 27 February was kept as Paardeberg Day until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-day">Armistice Day</a> replaced it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Paardeberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Second Boer War the Free State mobilised its commandos alongside the Transvaal from October 1899, besieging Kimberley and Ladysmith and fighting at Magersfontein and <b>Paardeberg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Military</a></li></ul></section>
